// === NAME - DESCRIPTION:  ===
// === SET QUESTION TYPE TO: multipart ===

// === COMMON CONTROL ===
$nCats = 4;
$catNames = array("Freshman", "Sophomore", "Junior", "Senior");
$nTotal = rand(120, 480);
$nTotal = 20 * floor($nTotal / 20);
$cnt = array();
$remaining = $nTotal;
for ($i = 0; $i < $nCats - 1; $i++) {
  $maxHere = $remaining - 20 * ($nCats - 1 - $i);
  $pick = 20 * rand(1, floor($maxHere / 20));
  $cnt[$i] = $pick;
  $remaining = $remaining - $pick;
}
$cnt[$nCats - 1] = $remaining;

$pct = array();
for ($i = 0; $i < $nCats; $i++) {
  $pct[$i] = 100 * $cnt[$i] / $nTotal;
}

$targetIdx = rand(0, $nCats - 1);
$targetName = $catNames[$targetIdx];
$targetCnt = $cnt[$targetIdx];
$targetPct = $pct[$targetIdx];

$answer[0] = $nTotal;
$answer[1] = $targetPct;
$answer[2] = 0;

$questions[2] = array(
  "The bars keep the same relative heights, so the shape of the graph is unchanged.",
  "The bars all become the same height, so the graph becomes flat.",
  "The tallest bar changes to a different category, so the shape changes.",
  "The bars keep the same heights in counts, so the graph looks identical in every way."
);

$anstypes = "number,number,choices";
$abstolerance = "0,0.05,0";

// === QUESTION TEXT ===
<p>A survey asked `$nTotal` students which class year they are in. The results are shown two ways below.</p>

<p><b>Graph 1: Number of students in each class year</b></p>
<table border="1" cellpadding="6" cellspacing="0">
<tr><th>Class year</th><th>Number of students</th></tr>
<tr><td>Freshman</td><td align="right">`$cnt[0]`</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sophomore</td><td align="right">`$cnt[1]`</td></tr>
<tr><td>Junior</td><td align="right">`$cnt[2]`</td></tr>
<tr><td>Senior</td><td align="right">`$cnt[3]`</td></tr>
</table>

<p><b>Graph 2: Percentage of students in each class year</b></p>
<table border="1" cellpadding="6" cellspacing="0">
<tr><th>Class year</th><th>Percent of students</th></tr>
<tr><td>Freshman</td><td align="right">`round($pct[0],1)`%</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sophomore</td><td align="right">`round($pct[1],1)`%</td></tr>
<tr><td>Junior</td><td align="right">`round($pct[2],1)`%</td></tr>
<tr><td>Senior</td><td align="right">`round($pct[3],1)`%</td></tr>
</table>

<p>(a) How many students were surveyed in total? $answerbox[0]</p>

<p>(b) What percentage of the students surveyed are $targetName? Give your answer as a percent, rounded to one decimal place. $answerbox[1]</p>

<p>(c) Which statement best explains why Graph 2 has the same shape as Graph 1? $answerbox[2]</p>

// === ANSWER ===
$solutionguide = '<p><b>Step 1 - Find the group size.</b> Add the counts in Graph 1: '
  . $cnt[0] . ' + ' . $cnt[1] . ' + ' . $cnt[2] . ' + ' . $cnt[3] . ' = ' . $nTotal . ' students.</p>'
  . '<p><b>Step 2 - Find the percentage for ' . $targetName . '.</b> There are ' . $targetCnt
  . ' ' . $targetName . ' out of ' . $nTotal . ', so the percentage is '
  . $targetCnt . ' / ' . $nTotal . ' = ' . round($targetPct, 4) . ', which rounds to '
  . round($targetPct, 1) . '%. This matches the entry in Graph 2.</p>'
  . '<p><b>Step 3 - Why the shape is unchanged.</b> Every count is divided by the same total, '
  . $nTotal . ', to get its percentage. Dividing every bar by one common number rescales the whole '
  . 'graph without changing how the bars compare to each other, so the tallest bar stays tallest and '
  . 'the relative heights are preserved. Only the axis labels change.</p>'
  . '<p><b>Answer:</b> (a) ' . $nTotal . ' students; (b) ' . round($targetPct, 1) . '%; '
  . '(c) The bars keep the same relative heights, so the shape of the graph is unchanged.</p>';

// === NAME - DESCRIPTION:  ===
// === SET QUESTION TYPE TO: multipart ===

// === COMMON CONTROL ===
// Build a 2x2 table with fixed grand total N so every expected count is exact.
$N = randfrom(array(80,100,120,160,200));

// Row totals: both even so halves are integers
$r1 = randfrom(array(30,40,50,60,70,80));
$r2 = $N - $r1;

// Column totals: both even so halves are integers
$c1 = randfrom(array(30,40,50,60,70,80,90,100));
$c2 = $N - $c1;

// Observed cell a11 must be a plausible integer strictly inside both margins
$lo = max(0, $r1 + $c1 - $N);
$hi = min($r1, $c1);
// keep away from the edges so the table is not degenerate
$lo = $lo + 2;
$hi = $hi - 2;
if ($hi < $lo) { $lo = max(0, $r1 + $c1 - $N); $hi = min($r1, $c1); }
$a11 = rand($lo, $hi);
$a12 = $r1 - $a11;
$a21 = $c1 - $a11;
$a22 = $r2 - $a21;

// Expected counts: E_ij = (row total * col total) / N
$e11 = $r1 * $c1 / $N;
$e12 = $r1 * $c2 / $N;
$e21 = $r2 * $c1 / $N;
$e22 = $r2 * $c2 / $N;

// Display values rounded to 2 decimals (they are exact at these N)
$e11d = round($e11, 2);
$e12d = round($e12, 2);
$e21d = round($e21, 2);
$e22d = round($e22, 2);

// Context labels
$rowLabels = array("Group A", "Group B");
$colLabels = array("Outcome 1", "Outcome 2");
$ri = rand(0,1);
$rowA = $rowLabels[$ri];
$rowB = $rowLabels[1-$ri];
$ci = rand(0,1);
$colA = $colLabels[$ci];
$colB = $colLabels[1-$ci];

$anstypes = "number,number,number";
$answer = array($e11d, $e12d, $e21d);
$abstolerance = array(0.02, 0.02, 0.02);

// === QUESTION TEXT ===
<p>A study cross-classifies `$N` subjects by two categorical variables. The observed counts and the marginal totals are shown below.</p>

<table border="1" cellpadding="6" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse:collapse;text-align:center;">
<tr><th></th><th>`$colA`</th><th>`$colB`</th><th>Row total</th></tr>
<tr><th style="text-align:left;">`$rowA`</th><td>`$a11`</td><td>`$a12`</td><td>`$r1`</td></tr>
<tr><th style="text-align:left;">`$rowB`</th><td>`$a21`</td><td>`$a22`</td><td>`$r2`</td></tr>
<tr><th style="text-align:left;">Column total</th><td>`$c1`</td><td>`$c2`</td><td>`$N`</td></tr>
</table>

<p>To test whether the two variables are independent, we compare the observed counts with the counts we would expect if the variables were independent. The expected count in a cell is</p>

<p style="text-align:center;">expected count = (row total &times; column total) / grand total.</p>

<p>Compute the expected counts for three of the four cells. Round each to two decimal places.</p>

<p>(a) Expected count for `$rowA` and `$colA`: $answerbox[0]</p>

<p>(b) Expected count for `$rowA` and `$colB`: $answerbox[1]</p>

<p>(c) Expected count for `$rowB` and `$colA`: $answerbox[2]</p>

// === ANSWER ===
$solutionguide = '<p><strong>Step 1 &mdash; Identify the margins.</strong> The grand total is ' . $N . '. The row totals are ' . $r1 . ' and ' . $r2 . '; the column totals are ' . $c1 . ' and ' . $c2 . '.</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p><strong>Step 2 &mdash; Apply the formula to each cell.</strong> Each expected count is (row total &times; column total) / grand total:</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<ul>';
$solutionguide .= '<li>' . $rowA . ' and ' . $colA . ': (' . $r1 . ' &times; ' . $c1 . ') / ' . $N . ' = ' . $e11d . '</li>';
$solutionguide .= '<li>' . $rowA . ' and ' . $colB . ': (' . $r1 . ' &times; ' . $c2 . ') / ' . $N . ' = ' . $e12d . '</li>';
$solutionguide .= '<li>' . $rowB . ' and ' . $colA . ': (' . $r2 . ' &times; ' . $c1 . ') / ' . $N . ' = ' . $e21d . '</li>';
$solutionguide .= '</ul>';
$solutionguide .= '<p><strong>Step 3 &mdash; Check.</strong> The four expected counts must add to the grand total. The fourth cell is (' . $r2 . ' &times; ' . $c2 . ') / ' . $N . ' = ' . $e22d . ', and ' . $e11d . ' + ' . $e12d . ' + ' . $e21d . ' + ' . $e22d . ' = ' . $N . '.</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p><strong>Answer:</strong> (a) ' . $e11d . ', (b) ' . $e12d . ', (c) ' . $e21d . '</p>';

// === NAME - DESCRIPTION: Addition Rule for a Union Probability - compute P(A OR B) by subtracting the overlap, and recognize why adding the two rates alone overcounts ===
// === SET QUESTION TYPE TO: multipart ===

// === COMMON CONTROL ===
$pa = rand(30,55)/100;
$pb = rand(30,55)/100;
$pab = rand(8,20)/100;
// keep the union a valid probability and the overlap genuinely smaller than each margin
if ($pa + $pb - $pab > 0.95) { $pab = $pa + $pb - 0.95; }
$punion = $pa + $pb - $pab;
$pnaive = $pa + $pb;
$overcount = $pab;

$paPct = round($pa*100);
$pbPct = round($pb*100);
$pabPct = round($pab*100);
$punionPct = round($punion*100, 2);
$pnaivePct = round($pnaive*100, 2);
$overcountPct = round($overcount*100, 2);

$anstypes = array("number","number","choices");
$answer = array();
$answer[0] = $punionPct;
$answer[1] = $pnaivePct;
$answer[2] = 1;

$questions = array();
$questions[2] = array(
  "Because the students who do both activities are counted twice when the two rates are added, so the overlap must be subtracted once.",
  "Because the two rates are not independent, so they cannot be added at all.",
  "Because the percentages must first be converted to decimals before adding.",
  "Because the overlap should be added a second time to correct for rounding."
);

$noshuffle = "questions";

$solutionguide = '<p><b>Step 1 - Identify the rule.</b> For any two events, the Addition Rule says</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p style="margin-left:2em;">`P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A and B)`</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p>The subtraction removes the students who were counted in both rates.</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p><b>Step 2 - Substitute the given values.</b></p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p style="margin-left:2em;">`P(A or B) = ' . $paPct . '% + ' . $pbPct . '% - ' . $pabPct . '% = ' . $punionPct . '%`</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p><b>Step 3 - Compare with the naive sum.</b> Adding the two rates alone gives</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p style="margin-left:2em;">`' . $paPct . '% + ' . $pbPct . '% = ' . $pnaivePct . '%`</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p>which is too large by exactly the overlap, `' . $overcountPct . '%`. The students who do both activities appear in the sport rate and again in the band rate, so they are counted twice. Subtracting `P(A and B)` once puts each student back to a single count.</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p><b>Answer:</b> (a) `' . $punionPct . '%` &nbsp; (b) `' . $pnaivePct . '%` &nbsp; (c) the overlap is double-counted, so it must be subtracted once.</p>';

// === QUESTION TEXT ===
<p>A survey of students at a large high school asked about two activities. The results are summarized below.</p>

<p style="margin-left:2em;">
`P(A) = $paPct%` &nbsp; is the probability that a randomly chosen student plays a school sport.<br />
`P(B) = $pbPct%` &nbsp; is the probability that a randomly chosen student is in the marching band.<br />
`P(A and B) = $pabPct%` &nbsp; is the probability that a randomly chosen student does both.
</p>

<p>(a) Find the probability that a randomly chosen student plays a school sport <b>or</b> is in the marching band. Report your answer as a percent, rounded to two decimal places if needed.</p>
<p>$answerbox[0] %</p>

<p>(b) A classmate computes the answer by adding the two rates: `$paPct% + $pbPct%`. What percent does the classmate get? Report as a percent, rounded to two decimal places if needed.</p>
<p>$answerbox[1] %</p>

<p>(c) The classmate's value is too large. Why does simply adding the two rates overcount?</p>
<p>$answerbox[2]</p>

// === NAME - DESCRIPTION:  ===
// === SET QUESTION TYPE TO: multipart ===

// === COMMON CONTROL ===
loadlibrary("stats");

// ---- Part (a): right-tail area -> left area for invNorm ----
$rtPct = rand(2,10);              // right tail percent, e.g. 5
$rtArea = $rtPct/100;             // right tail as a decimal
$leftArea = 1 - $rtArea;          // left area to give invNorm
$leftAreaR = round($leftArea, 2);

// ---- Part (b): the critical z for that right-tail area ----
$zCrit = round(invnormalcdf($leftArea, 0, 1), 3);

// ---- Part (c): z that bounds the middle percent ----
$midPct = randfrom(array(80,90,95,98));
$midArea = $midPct/100;
$tailArea = (1 - $midArea)/2;     // area in each tail
$zMid = round(invnormalcdf(1 - $tailArea, 0, 1), 3);

// ---- Part (d): multiple choice on which area to use ----
$questions[3] = array(
  "The area to the left of the critical z, which is 1 minus the right-tail area",
  "The right-tail area itself, unchanged",
  "The area to the left of the critical z, which is the right-tail area divided by 2",
  "The area between the two critical z values, which is 1 minus twice the right-tail area"
);
$answer[3] = 0;

$anstypes = "number,number,number,choices";
$answer[0] = $leftAreaR;
$answer[1] = $zCrit;
$answer[2] = $zMid;

$abstolerance[0] = 0.005;
$abstolerance[1] = 0.002;
$abstolerance[2] = 0.002;

$noshuffle = "questions";

// === QUESTION TEXT ===
<p>A researcher is building a confidence interval and needs a <b>critical z</b> for a
one-sided test whose <b>right-tail area</b> is `$rtPct`%.</p>

<p><b>(a)</b> The function invNorm takes an area to the <i>left</i> of the z you want.
What left area should you give invNorm to get this critical z? Give a decimal.<br />
$answerbox[0]</p>

<p><b>(b)</b> Use that area to find the critical z. Round to three decimal places.<br />
`z =` $answerbox[1]</p>

<p><b>(c)</b> A different interval must capture the middle `$midPct`% of the standard normal
curve. Find the positive z that bounds this middle region. Round to three decimal places.<br />
`z =` $answerbox[2]</p>

<p><b>(d)</b> Which statement best explains what area you hand to invNorm when you are given a
right-tail area?<br />
$answerbox[3]</p>

// === ANSWER ===
$solutionguide = '<p><b>Step 1 — Decide which area invNorm wants.</b> invNorm always takes the area to the LEFT of the z you are looking for. A right-tail area of ' . $rtPct . '% means ' . $rtArea . ' of the curve sits to the right of the critical z, so the area to the left is 1 - ' . $rtArea . ' = ' . $leftAreaR . '.</p>
<p><b>Step 2 — Find the critical z.</b> invNorm(' . $leftAreaR . ', 0, 1) = ' . $zCrit . '. This z has ' . $rtPct . '% of the area to its right, as required.</p>
<p><b>Step 3 — Bound the middle ' . $midPct . '%.</b> The middle ' . $midPct . '% leaves ' . round(100*$tailArea,1) . '% in each tail, so the area to the left of the upper z is 1 - ' . round($tailArea,4) . ' = ' . round(1-$tailArea,4) . '. Then invNorm(' . round(1-$tailArea,4) . ', 0, 1) = ' . $zMid . '. By symmetry the lower bound is -' . $zMid . '.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> (a) ' . $leftAreaR . '; (b) z = ' . $zCrit . '; (c) z = ' . $zMid . '; (d) the area to the left of the critical z, which is 1 minus the right-tail area.</p>';

