// === QUESTION TEXT ===
<p>The scatterplot below shows data with a least-squares regression line drawn through it.</p>
<p>Which statement best describes the relationship between the two variables?</p>
<p>$answerbox</p>

// === ANSWER ===
$solutionguide = 'The correct answer is: The points show scatter around a roughly linear trend. The points follow the upward direction of the line, but they are not exactly on it -- some lie above the line and some below, which is what scatter around a linear trend looks like. A strong curved pattern would bend away from a straight line, no pattern would look like a random cloud, and a linear trend with no scatter would have every point exactly on the line.';

// === NAME - DESCRIPTION: Reading a Study - Classify a described study as observational or experimental, name a plausible source of bias or confounding, and state which conclusion the design does and does not justify ===
// === SET QUESTION TYPE TO: multipart ===

// === COMMON CONTROL ===
$scenarios = array(
  array(
    "desc" => "A researcher at a large university wants to know whether drinking coffee before an exam improves scores. She asks 400 students in the library on exam day whether they drank coffee that morning, then compares the exam scores of the coffee drinkers with those of the non-drinkers.",
    "type" => "observational",
    "bias" => "Students who choose to drink coffee before an exam may already differ from those who do not -- for example, they may be more anxious, more sleep-deprived, or more experienced with caffeine -- and those differences, not the coffee, could explain any gap in scores.",
    "justified" => "Among students like these, coffee drinking and exam scores are associated.",
    "notjustified" => "Drinking coffee causes higher exam scores."
  ),
  array(
    "desc" => "A physical therapist randomly assigns 60 patients recovering from knee surgery to either a new exercise program or the standard program. After eight weeks she compares how far each patient can walk in six minutes.",
    "type" => "experimental",
    "bias" => "Patients who know they are on the new program may push harder, and the therapist measuring the distance may unconsciously favor one group; blinding the measurer would reduce this.",
    "justified" => "For patients like these, the new program causes a change in how far they can walk in six minutes.",
    "notjustified" => "The new program would work equally well for all people of all ages."
  ),
  array(
    "desc" => "A city health department surveys 1,200 residents by calling landline telephone numbers listed in the phone book, asking how many minutes per week they exercise.",
    "type" => "observational",
    "bias" => "People with only a cell phone, or with unlisted numbers, are left out of the frame, and those who answer a landline during the day may differ systematically from the rest of the city.",
    "justified" => "The residents reached by this survey reported the exercise times they reported.",
    "notjustified" => "The average exercise time of all city residents is the average of these 1,200 responses."
  ),
  array(
    "desc" => "A botanist grows 40 tomato plants in a greenhouse. He gives fertilizer to the 20 plants nearest the door and no fertilizer to the other 20, then compares the number of tomatoes each plant produces.",
    "type" => "experimental",
    "bias" => "The plants nearest the door get different light and temperature than the rest of the greenhouse, so the door position is confounded with the fertilizer.",
    "justified" => "In this greenhouse, the fertilized plants and the unfertilized plants differed in tomato count.",
    "notjustified" => "Fertilizer causes more tomatoes on any tomato plant anywhere."
