$solutionguide = '<p><b>Step 1 &mdash; Write the constraints.</b> Wax: `' . $a1 . 'x + ' . $b1 . 'y <= ' . $c1 . '`. Labor: `' . $a2 . 'x + ' . $b2 . 'y <= ' . $c2 . '`. With `x >= 0`, `y >= 0`.</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p><b>Step 2 &mdash; Add slacks and set up the initial tableau.</b> Introduce `s_1` and `s_2`:</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p style="text-align:center">`' . $a1 . 'x + ' . $b1 . 'y + s_1 = ' . $c1 . '`</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p style="text-align:center">`' . $a2 . 'x + ' . $b2 . 'y + s_2 = ' . $c2 . '`</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p style="text-align:center">`P - ' . $p1 . 'x - ' . $p2 . 'y = 0`</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p>The initial basic solution is `x = 0`, `y = 0`, `s_1 = ' . $c1 . '`, `s_2 = ' . $c2 . '`, `P = 0`.</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p><b>Step 3 &mdash; Pivot to the optimum.</b> The most negative entry in the profit row is the pivot column. Pivoting on the smaller ratio of right-hand side to pivot-column entry gives the entering and leaving variables. After pivoting, both resource constraints are tight at the optimum, so `s_1 = s_2 = 0` and the basic variables are `x` and `y`.</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p>Solving the two binding equations simultaneously gives `x = ' . $xopt . '` and `y = ' . $yopt . '`.</p>';
