$solutionguide = '<p><strong>Step 1 — State the rule.</strong> Chebyshev\'s Rule says that for any data set, regardless of shape, at least `1 - 1/k^2` of the data lies within `k` standard deviations of the mean, for any `k > 1`.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2 — Part (a), `k = 2`.</strong> `1 - 1/2^2 = 1 - 1/4 = 3/4 = 0.75`, so at least <strong>75%</strong> of the data lies within 2 standard deviations of the mean.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3 — Part (b), `k = 3`.</strong> `1 - 1/3^2 = 1 - 1/9 = 8/9 ≈ 0.8889`, so at least <strong>88.89%</strong> of the data lies within 3 standard deviations of the mean.</p>
<p><strong>Step 4 — Part (c), `k = 4.5`.</strong> `1 - 1/4.5^2 = 1 - 1/20.25 = 1 - 0.049383 ≈ 0.9506`, so at least <strong>95.06%</strong> of the data lies within 4.5 standard deviations of the mean.</p>
<p><strong>Step 5 — Part (d).</strong> The rule is a floor, not an exact count. It guarantees that <em>no less than</em> 75% of the data lies within 2 standard deviations, but the actual percentage can be higher and depends on the shape of the distribution. A distribution with heavy tails and a tall center, such as one with most values clustered near the mean and a few extreme outliers, can put far more than 75% of its data within 2 standard deviations. The claim of an exact 75% is therefore wrong.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> (a) 75%, (b) 88.89%, (c) 95.06%, (d) the second statement: the rule gives a guaranteed minimum, and the true percentage may be higher.</p>';

