// === QUESTION TEXT ===
<p>Use the <strong>power property of logarithms</strong>: for any base $base and any real number $p,</p>
<p style="text-align:center">`log_b(x^p) = p log_b(x)`</p>

<p><strong>(a)</strong> Rewrite `log_$base(x^$p)` as a coefficient times `log_$base(x)`. What is the coefficient?</p>
<p>$answerbox[0]</p>

<p><strong>(b)</strong> Expand `log_$base( (x^$m y^$n) / z^$p )` completely. Enter the three coefficients in order: the coefficient of `log_$base(x)`, then of `log_$base(y)`, then of `log_$base(z)` (use a negative number for the subtracted term).</p>
<p>$answerbox[1] $answerbox[2] $answerbox[3]</p>

<p><strong>(c)</strong> Condense `$aC log_$base(x) + $cC log_$base(y)` into a single logarithm `log_$base(x^A y^C)`. Enter the exponent A on x, then the exponent C on y.</p>
<p>$answerbox[4] $answerbox[5]</p>

<p><strong>(d)</strong> Evaluate `log_$base($base^$kD)`.</p>
<p>$answerbox[6]</p>

<p><strong>(e)</strong> Rewrite `log_$base(x^$rE)` as a coefficient times `log_$base(x)`. What is the coefficient?</p>
<p>$answerbox[7]</p>

