$solutionguide = '<p><strong>Step 1 — Power property.</strong> The power property says `log_b(x^p) = p log_b(x)`: an exponent inside a logarithm comes out front as a multiplier.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2 — Part (a).</strong> `log_' . $base . '(x^' . $p . ') = ' . $p . ' log_' . $base . '(x)`, so the coefficient is ' . $p . '.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3 — Part (b).</strong> Use the product and quotient rules first, then the power rule: `log_' . $base . '((x^' . $m . ' y^' . $n . ')/z^' . $p . ')) = ' . $m . ' log_' . $base . '(x) + ' . $n . ' log_' . $base . '(y) - ' . $p . ' log_' . $base . '(z)`. The coefficients are ' . $m . ', ' . $n . ', and -' . $p . '.</p>
<p><strong>Step 4 — Part (c).</strong> A coefficient becomes an exponent: `' . $aC . ' log_' . $base . '(x) + ' . $cC . ' log_' . $base . '(y) = log_' . $base . '(x^' . $aC . ') + log_' . $base . '(y^' . $cC . ') = log_' . $base . '(x^' . $aC . ' y^' . $cC . ')`. So A = ' . $aC . ' and C = ' . $cC . '.</p>
<p><strong>Step 5 — Part (d).</strong> `log_' . $base . '(' . $base . '^' . $kD . ') = ' . $kD . ' log_' . $base . '(' . $base . ') = ' . $kD . ' * 1 = ' . $kD . '`.</p>
<p><strong>Step 6 — Part (e).</strong> `log_' . $base . '(x^' . $rE . ') = ' . $rE . ' log_' . $base . '(x)`, so the coefficient is ' . $rE . '.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> (a) ' . $p . '; (b) ' . $m . ', ' . $n . ', -' . $p . '; (c) A = ' . $aC . ', C = ' . $cC . '; (d) ' . $kD . '; (e) ' . $rE . '.</p>';

// === NAME - DESCRIPTION: Probability That a Sum Is Greater Than a Value - P(Sigma x > c): given a population mean, SD, and sample size, find the probability that the SUM of n observations exceeds a cutoff, using the sampling distribution of the sum ===
// === SET QUESTION TYPE TO: multipart ===

// === COMMON CONTROL ===
$mu = rand(20,90);
$sigma = rand(4,18);
$nlist = array(9,12,16,20,25,30,36,49,64);
$n = $nlist[rand(0,8)];
$k = rand(1,3);          // how many SDs above the mean the cutoff sits
$c = $mu*$n + $k*$sigma*sqrt($n);
$c = round($c);
// recompute the z from the ROUNDED cutoff so the key matches what the student reads
$sumMean = $mu*$n;
$sumSD = $sigma*sqrt($n);
$z = ($c - $sumMean)/$sumSD;
loadlibrary("stats");
$p = 1 - normalcdf($z,0,1);
$p3 = round($p,4);
$z2 = round($z,2);
$sumMeanDisp = round($sumMean,2);
$sumSDDisp = round($sumSD,2);

$anstypes = "number,number,number,choices";
$answer[0] = $sumMean;
$answer[1] = $sumSD;
$answer[2] = $p;
$questions[3] = array(
  "The distribution of the sum is exactly Normal only if the population itself is Normal; otherwise it is approximately Normal for large n.",
  "The distribution of the sum is exactly Normal for any sample size, whatever the population looks like.",
  "The distribution of the sum is never Normal, because a sum of observations cannot be Normal.",
  "The distribution of the sum is Normal only when the sample size is small."
);
$answer[3] = 0;

// === QUESTION TEXT ===
<p>A population has mean `\mu = $mu` and standard deviation `\sigma = $sigma`. A random sample of `n = $n` observations is taken, and the <b>sum</b> of the observations, `\Sigma x`, is recorded.</p>

<p>(a) Find the mean of the sampling distribution of the sum `\Sigma x`.</p>
<p>`\mu_{\Sigma x} =` $answerbox[0]</p>

<p>(b) Find the standard deviation of the sampling distribution of the sum `\Sigma x`. Round to two decimal places.</p>
<p>`\sigma_{\Sigma x} =` $answerbox[1]</p>

<p>(c) Find the probability that the sum of the `$n` observations is greater than `$c`. Round to four decimal places.</p>
<p>`P(\Sigma x > $c) =` $answerbox[2]</p>

<p>(d) Which statement best describes when the sampling distribution of the sum may be treated as Normal?</p>
<p>$answerbox[3]</p>

// === ANSWER ===
$solutionguide = '<p><b>Step 1 — Mean of the sum.</b> The mean of a sum of `n` independent observations is `n` times the population mean:</p>
<p>`\mu_{\Sigma x} = n\mu = ' . $n . ' \cdot ' . $mu . ' = ' . $sumMeanDisp . '`</p>
<p><b>Step 2 — Standard deviation of the sum.</b> Variances add for independent observations, so the variance of the sum is `n\sigma^2` and the standard deviation is `\sqrt{n}` times `\sigma`:</p>
<p>`\sigma_{\Sigma x} = \sqrt{n}\,\sigma = \sqrt{' . $n . '} \cdot ' . $sigma . ' = ' . $sumSDDisp . '`</p>
<p><b>Step 3 — Standardize and use the Normal table.</b> The cutoff is `' . $c . '`, so</p>
<p>`z = \frac{' . $c . ' - ' . $sumMeanDisp . '}{' . $sumSDDisp . '} = ' . $z2 . '`</p>
<p>We want the area to the right of this z:</p>
<p>`P(\Sigma x > ' . $c . ') = P(Z > ' . $z2 . ') = 1 - ' . round(1-$p,4) . ' = ' . $p3 . '`</p>
<p><b>Step 4 — Normality.</b> A sum of independent observations is exactly Normal when the population is Normal. When the population is not Normal, the Central Limit Theorem makes the sum approximately Normal once `n` is large, which is why the calculation above is trusted here with `n = ' . $n . '`.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> (a) `' . $sumMeanDisp . '`, (b) `' . $sumSDDisp . '`, (c) `' . $p3 . '`, (d) the first statement.</p>';

