// === QUESTION TEXT ===
<p>A workshop presses ceramic tiles. The thickness of a single tile varies, and across all the tiles the workshop makes, thickness is <b>strongly right skewed</b> with mean `$mu` millimeters and standard deviation `$sigma` millimeters.</p>

<p>A quality inspector pulls a batch of `$n` tiles at random and records `S`, the <b>sum</b> of the `$n` thicknesses. Consider the sampling distribution of `S` over all possible batches of `$n` tiles.</p>

<p>Four students were asked to describe the shape, the center, and the spread of the sampling distribution of `S`. Their answers are below. Grade each answer with the checklist.</p>

<p><b>Grading checklist</b></p>
<ul>
<li>The center is wrong: it uses the population mean instead of n times the population mean.</li>
<li>The spread is wrong: it divides the population standard deviation by the square root of n, which is the standard error of the sample mean and not of the sum.</li>
<li>The spread is wrong: it multiplies the population standard deviation by n instead of by the square root of n.</li>
<li>The shape is wrong: it calls the distribution exactly Normal, when the central limit theorem only makes it approximately Normal.</li>
<li>Nothing is wrong: this response is correct.</li>
</ul>

<p><b>Response A:</b> $respA<br>
Which checklist item applies to Response A? $answerbox[0]</p>

<p><b>Response B:</b> $respB<br>
Which checklist item applies to Response B? $answerbox[1]</p>

<p><b>Response C:</b> $respC<br>
Which checklist item applies to Response C? $answerbox[2]</p>

<p><b>Response D:</b> $respD<br>
Which checklist item applies to Response D? $answerbox[3]</p>

// === ANSWER ===
$solutionguide = '<p><b>Step 1: the center.</b> A sum of ' . $n . ' independent observations has mean ' . $n . ' times the population mean, so the center of the sampling distribution of the sum is ' . $n . ' times ' . $mu . ' = ' . $nmu . ' millimeters. An answer that gives the population mean ' . $mu . ' has described a single observation, not a sum.</p>

<p><b>Step 2: the spread.</b> A sum of ' . $n . ' independent observations has variance ' . $n . ' times the population variance, so its standard deviation is the square root of ' . $n . ' times the population standard deviation: ' . $rootN . ' times ' . $sigma . ' = ' . $sigRootN . ' millimeters. Dividing instead, ' . $sigma . ' divided by ' . $rootN . ' = ' . $sigOverRootN . ', gives the standard error of the sample mean, which is the wrong quantity for a sum, and multiplying by ' . $n . ' to get ' . $sigTimesN . ' overstates the spread.</p>

<p><b>Step 3: the shape.</b> The population is strongly right skewed, so the sum is only approximately Normal at ' . $n . ' observations. Saying the distribution is exactly Normal is a real error, not a rounding quibble.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Response A: ' . $verdictLabel[$finalCodes[0]] . '. Response B: ' . $verdictLabel[$finalCodes[1]] . '. Response C: ' . $verdictLabel[$finalCodes[2]] . '. Response D: ' . $verdictLabel[$finalCodes[3]] . '.</p>';

