<p><b>c.</b> How much more money does Account B hold than Account A after $t years? $answerbox[2]</p>

<p><b>d.</b> Which statement best explains why Account B ends up with more money than Account A? $answerbox[3]</p>

// === ANSWER ===
$solutionguide = '<p><b>Step 1 - Grow Account A with annual compounding.</b> Compounding once a year means one period per year, so t years is t compounding periods. `A = P(1 + r)^t = ' . $P . '(1 + ' . $r . ')^' . $t . ' = ' . $AaD . '`</p><p><b>Step 2 - Grow Account B with monthly compounding.</b> The annual rate is split into 12 equal monthly rates, and 12t compounding periods occur. `A = P(1 + r/12)^(12t) = ' . $P . '(1 + ' . $mr . ')^' . $n . ' = ' . $AmD . '`</p><p><b>Step 3 - Compare the two balances.</b> Subtract the annual balance from the monthly balance: `' . $AmD . ' - ' . $AaD . ' = ' . $AdiffD . '`, so Account B holds about $' . $AdiffD . ' more than Account A.</p><p><b>Step 4 - Explain the gap.</b> Both accounts use the same annual rate and the same principal. The difference is how often the interest is credited. Account B has interest added to the balance 12 times a year, so each credit starts earning interest during the very next month, while Account A waits a full year for the first credit. More frequent compounding therefore produces a slightly larger balance without any change in the quoted annual rate.</p><p><b>Answer:</b> Account A holds $' . $AaD . '; Account B holds $' . $AmD . '; Account B is ahead by $' . $AdiffD . '; the reason is that interest is credited more often, so interest begins earning interest sooner.</p>';

