<p>Simplex Candle Shop makes two kinds of candles. Each <b>taper candle</b> uses $a1 ounces of soy wax and $b1 minutes of hand-dipping labor, and earns a profit of $p dollars. Each <b>pillar candle</b> uses $a2 ounces of soy wax and $b2 minutes at the pouring station, and earns a profit of $q dollars.</p>

<p>Each day the shop has $c1 ounces of soy wax and $c2 minutes of labor available. Let `x` be the number of taper candles and `y` the number of pillar candles made in one day.</p>

<p><b>a.</b> Write the linear programming model for this situation, then solve it with the simplex method. How many of each candle should the shop make in a day to maximize profit? Enter whole numbers.<br />
Taper candles: $answerbox[0] &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Pillar candles: $answerbox[1]</p>

<p><b>b.</b> What is the maximum daily profit, in dollars? $answerbox[2]</p>

<p><b>c.</b> In the final simplex tableau, what do the values of the two slack variables say about the shop resources? $answerbox[3]</p>

