$solutionguide = "<p><b>Step 1. Write the model.</b></p>
<p>Maximize profit `P = $p x + $q y`</p>
<p>subject to the wax limit `$a1 x + $a2 y <= $c1` and the labor limit `$b1 x + $b2 y <= $c2`, with `x >= 0` and `y >= 0`.</p>
<p><b>Step 2. Add a slack variable to each constraint.</b></p>
<p>`$a1 x + $a2 y + s_1 = $c1`</p>
<p>`$b1 x + $b2 y + s_2 = $c2`</p>
<p>In the initial simplex tableau the objective row reads `-$p` under `x` and `-$q` under `y`. Because `$q > $p`, the entry `-$q` is the more negative of the two, so pillar candles are the entering variable and the method pivots from there.</p>
<p><b>Step 3. Pivoting stops when no entry in the objective row is negative.</b> That happens at the corner where both constraints are tight, so solve the two constraint equations together:</p>
<p>`$a1 x + $a2 y = $c1` and `$b1 x + $b2 y = $c2`</p>
<p>which gives `x = $x0` and `y = $y0`.</p>
<p>Check the resources at that corner: wax used is `$a1 cdot $x0 + $a2 cdot $y0 = $waxused` ounces, and labor used is `$b1 cdot $x0 + $b2 cdot $y0 = $labused` minutes. Both match what is available, so `s_1 = 0` and `s_2 = 0` and neither resource is left over.</p>
<p><b>Step 4. Compare the corner points of the feasible region.</b></p>
<p>The corner `x = 0, y = 0` gives `P = 0`.</p>
<p>The corner `x = $xi, y = 0` gives `P = $profx`.</p>
<p>The corner `x = 0, y = $yi` gives `P = $profy`.</p>
<p>The corner `x = $x0, y = $y0` gives `P = $maxprofit`, the largest of the four.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> the shop should make $x0 taper candles and $y0 pillar candles each day, for a maximum daily profit of $maxprofit dollars. Because both slack variables end at zero, all of the wax and all of the labor are used up.</p>";

