// === NAME - DESCRIPTION: Camila vs Rosa settlement: a count table and a percent table of the same claimants must give the same share above a boundary, and moving the boundary changes who is included ===
// === SET QUESTION TYPE TO: multipart ===

// === COMMON CONTROL ===
$anstypes = array("number","number","choices","number");
$abstolerance = 0.01;

// 8 claim amounts, 1 to 8 thousand dollars. Total claimants fixed at 50 so
// every displayed share is an exact whole percent and the shares must add up.
$counts = array();
for ($k=0;$k<8;$k++) {
  $counts[$k] = 4;
}
for ($j=0;$j<18;$j++) {
  $pick = rand(0,7);
  $counts[$pick] = $counts[$pick] + 1;
}
$totalc = 0;
for ($k=0;$k<8;$k++) {
  $totalc = $totalc + $counts[$k];
}

// above the boundary: amounts 4,5,6,7,8.  at the boundary: amount 3.
$numabove = $counts[3] + $counts[4] + $counts[5] + $counts[6] + $counts[7];
$numeq = $counts[2];
$numatleast = $numabove + $numeq;

$pctabove = round(100*$numabove/$totalc);
$pctatleast = round(100*$numatleast/$totalc);

$rows1 = "";
$rows2 = "";
for ($k=0;$k<8;$k++) {
  $amt = $k + 1;
  $ct = $counts[$k];
  $pc = round(100*$ct/$totalc);
  $rows1 = $rows1 . "<tr><td style='text-align:center'>" . $amt . "</td><td style='text-align:center'>" . $ct . "</td></tr>";
  $rows2 = $rows2 . "<tr><td style='text-align:center'>" . $amt . "</td><td style='text-align:center'>" . $pc . "%</td></tr>";
}
$rows1 = $rows1 . "<tr><td style='text-align:center'><b>Total</b></td><td style='text-align:center'><b>" . $totalc . "</b></td></tr>";
$rows2 = $rows2 . "<tr><td style='text-align:center'><b>Total</b></td><td style='text-align:center'><b>100%</b></td></tr>";

$table1 = "<table border='1' style='border-collapse:collapse'><tr><th>Claim amount in thousands of dollars</th><th>Number of claimants</th></tr>" . $rows1 . "</table>";
$table2 = "<table border='1' style='border-collapse:collapse'><tr><th>Claim amount in thousands of dollars</th><th>Share of claimants</th></tr>" . $rows2 . "</table>";

$opt1 = "Both tables describe the same claimants and both cut at the same place, so the same people fall above the cut and the share is that count divided by the total.";
$opt2 = "Three thousand dollars is the median claim amount, and a median always splits the claimants exactly in half.";
$opt3 = "Rosa divided each count by 100 instead of by the total number of claimants.";
$opt4 = "A claimant at exactly 3 thousand dollars is counted above the boundary in one table and below it in the other, so the two mistakes cancel.";

$questions[2] = array($opt1, $opt2, $opt3, $opt4);
$answer[0] = $numabove;
$answer[1] = $pctabove;
$answer[2] = 0;
$answer[3] = $pctatleast;

// === QUESTION TEXT ===
<p>Camila and Rosa are both summarizing the same group of $totalc claimants in a class-action settlement. Each claimant received a whole number of thousands of dollars, from 1 to 8 thousand. Camila built a table of counts and Rosa built a table of shares.</p>

<p><b>Table 1: Camila, counts</b></p>
$table1
<br>
<p><b>Table 2: Rosa, shares</b></p>
$table2

<p><b>Part a.</b> Using the count table, how many claimants received more than 3 thousand dollars? $answerbox[0]</p>
<p>///</p>
<p><b>Part b.</b> Using the share table, what share of the claimants received more than 3 thousand dollars? Give a percent, not a decimal. $answerbox[1]</p>
<p>///</p>
<p><b>Part c.</b> The count in part a and the share in part b describe the same claimants. Why do the two tables have to give the same share? $answerbox[2]</p>
<p>///</p>
<p><b>Part d.</b> Now move the boundary. What share of the claimants received at least 3 thousand dollars? Give a percent. $answerbox[3]</p>

