$solutionguide = '<p><b>Step 1 - Read the boundary.</b> More than 3 thousand dollars means the claim amounts 4, 5, 6, 7 and 8 thousand. A claimant who received exactly 3 thousand dollars is not in that group.</p>' .
'<p><b>Step 2 - Add the counts.</b> The count table gives ' . $counts[3] . ' + ' . $counts[4] . ' + ' . $counts[5] . ' + ' . $counts[6] . ' + ' . $counts[7] . ' = ' . $numabove . ' claimants.</p>' .
'<p><b>Step 3 - Add the shares.</b> The share table gives ' . round(100*$counts[3]/$totalc) . ' + ' . round(100*$counts[4]/$totalc) . ' + ' . round(100*$counts[5]/$totalc) . ' + ' . round(100*$counts[6]/$totalc) . ' + ' . round(100*$counts[7]/$totalc) . ' = ' . $pctabove . ' percent. Both tables describe the same ' . $totalc . ' claimants and both cut at the same place, so exactly the same people sit above the cut. The share is that count divided by ' . $totalc . ' and multiplied by 100, which is why the two answers have to agree.</p>' .
'<p><b>Step 4 - Move the boundary.</b> At least 3 thousand dollars also includes the ' . $numeq . ' claimants who received exactly 3 thousand, so ' . $numabove . ' + ' . $numeq . ' = ' . $numatleast . ' claimants, and 100 times ' . $numatleast . ' divided by ' . $totalc . ' is ' . $pctatleast . ' percent. The boundary moved by one amount and the share moved with it.</p>' .
'<p><b>Answer:</b> part a is ' . $numabove . ' claimants, part b is ' . $pctabove . ' percent, part c is the first choice, and part d is ' . $pctatleast . ' percent.</p>';

