$solutionguide = '<p><b>Step 1 - Fix the boundary.</b> More than 3 means 4 or more, so a plant with exactly 3 tomatoes sits outside the group in both tables. The numerator for each table is that table 4 or more count.</p><p><b>Step 2 - Camila.</b> Her 4 or more column holds ' . $d1 . ' plants out of ' . $N1 . ', so her share is ' . $d1 . ' divided by ' . $N1 . ', which is ' . $share1d . '.</p><p><b>Step 3 - Rosa.</b> Her 4 or more column holds ' . $d2 . ' plants out of ' . $N2 . ', so her share is ' . $d2 . ' divided by ' . $N2 . ', which is ' . $share2d . '. The two shares agree even though the counts ' . $d1 . ' and ' . $d2 . ' differ and the totals ' . $N1 . ' and ' . $N2 . ' differ. Comparing the raw counts would have been misleading, because the two plots are not the same size.</p><p><b>Step 4 - Move the boundary and watch the tie break.</b> Under 3 or more the plants with exactly 3 tomatoes join the group: ' . $c1 . ' of Camila plants and ' . $c2 . ' of Rosa plants. The shares become ' . $incl1 . ' and ' . $incl2 . ', so ' . $winner . ' is ahead. None of the data changed, only the boundary did. That is why the boundary, not the counts, is what explains the agreement in parts a and b.</p><p><b>Answer:</b> part a ' . $share1d . ', part b ' . $share2d . ', part c the boundary keeps the exactly 3 plants out of the numerator in both tables so each share is counted share against share rather than count against count, part d ' . $winner . '.</p>';

// === NAME - DESCRIPTION: Matrix Inverse & Equation (3x3) - Find A^(-1) then solve AX=B using the inverse method ===
// === SET QUESTION TYPE TO: multipart ===

// === COMMON CONTROL ===
$l21 = randfrom(array(-1,0,1));
$l31 = randfrom(array(-1,0,1));
$l32 = randfrom(array(-1,0,1));
if ($l21==0 && $l31==0 && $l32==0) { $l21 = 1; }

$u12 = randfrom(array(-1,0,1));
$u13 = randfrom(array(-1,0,1));
$u23 = randfrom(array(-1,0,1));
if ($u12==0 && $u13==0 && $u23==0) { $u23 = 1; }

// A = L*U with unit-triangular L and U, so det(A) = 1 and A^(-1) is an integer matrix
$A11 = 1;
$A12 = $u12;
$A13 = $u13;
$A21 = $l21;
$A22 = $l21*$u12 + 1;
$A23 = $l21*$u13 + $u23;
$A31 = $l31;
$A32 = $l31*$u12 + $l32;
$A33 = $l31*$u13 + $l32*$u23 + 1;

// U^(-1)
$U11 = 1;  $U12 = -$u12;  $U13 = $u12*$u23 - $u13;
$U21 = 0;  $U22 = 1;      $U23 = -$u23;
$U31 = 0;  $U32 = 0;      $U33 = 1;

// L^(-1)
$L11 = 1;                 $L12 = 0;   $L13 = 0;
$L21 = -$l21;             $L22 = 1;   $L23 = 0;
$L31 = $l21*$l32 - $l31;  $L32 = -$l32;  $L33 = 1;

// A^(-1) = U^(-1) * L^(-1)
$I11 = $U11*$L11 + $U12*$L21 + $U13*$L31;
$I12 = $U11*$L12 + $U12*$L22 + $U13*$L32;
$I13 = $U11*$L13 + $U12*$L23 + $U13*$L33;
$I21 = $U21*$L11 + $U22*$L21 + $U23*$L31;
$I22 = $U21*$L12 + $U22*$L22 + $U23*$L32;
$I23 = $U21*$L13 + $U22*$L23 + $U23*$L33;
$I31 = $U31*$L11 + $U32*$L21 + $U33*$L31;
$I32 = $U31*$L12 + $U32*$L22 + $U33*$L32;
$I33 = $U31*$L13 + $U32*$L23 + $U33*$L33;

// the solution vector is chosen first, then B = A X
$x1 = randfrom(array(-3,-2,-1,1,2,3));
$x2 = randfrom(array(-3,-2,-1,1,2,3));
$x3 = randfrom(array(-3,-2,-1,1,2,3));

$b1 = $A11*$x1 + $A12*$x2 + $A13*$x3;
$b2 = $A21*$x1 + $A22*$x2 + $A23*$x3;
$b3 = $A31*$x1 + $A32*$x2 + $A33*$x3;

$Astr = "[($A11,$A12,$A13),($A21,$A22,$A23),($A31,$A32,$A33)]";
$Bstr = "[($b1),($b2),($b3)]";
$Xstr = "($x1,$x2,$x3)";
$AinvStr = "[($I11,$I12,$I13),($I21,$I22,$I23),($I31,$I32,$I33)]";

$anstypes = "matrix,ntuple";
$answersize[0] = "3,3";
$displayformat[1] = "vector";
$answer[0] = $AinvStr;
$answer[1] = $Xstr;

// === QUESTION TEXT ===
<p>Let</p>
<p style="text-align:center">`A = $Astr` and `B = $Bstr`.</p>
<p>(a) Find `A^(-1)`. Enter the nine entries of the inverse.</p>
<p>$answerbox[0]</p>
<p>(b) Use the inverse to solve `AX = B` for `X`. Enter `X` as an ordered triple.</p>
<p>$answerbox[1]</p>

// === ANSWER ===
$solutionguide = '<p><strong>Step 1 - The determinant.</strong> Expanding by cofactors, `det(A) = 1`. Because the determinant is not zero, `A^(-1)` exists.</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p><strong>Step 2 - The inverse.</strong> Form the matrix of cofactors, transpose it to get the adjugate, and divide by the determinant: `A^(-1) = (adj A)/(det A) = ' . $AinvStr . '`.</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p><strong>Step 3 - Solve the system.</strong> Since `A X = B`, multiply both sides on the left by `A^(-1)`: `X = A^(-1) B = ' . $Xstr . '`.</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p><strong>Step 4 - Check.</strong> Multiplying `A` by this vector returns `' . $Bstr . '`, which is `B`. Multiplying `A` by the matrix found in part (a) returns the 3 by 3 identity matrix.</p>';

