<p><strong>d.</strong> How many display slots are left empty at the optimal plan? $answerbox[3]</p>
<p><strong>e.</strong> At the optimal plan the wax constraint and the labor constraint are both binding, while the display case constraint is not. Which statement correctly describes the situation? $answerbox[4]</p>

// === ANSWER ===
$solutionguide = '<p><strong>Step 1. Write the linear program.</strong> With x for votive candles and y for pillar candles, the daily problem is:</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p>Maximize `P = ' . $aProfit . 'x + ' . $bProfit . 'y`</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p>subject to `' . $waxV . 'x + ' . $waxP . 'y <= ' . $waxAvail . '` for wax, `' . $labV . 'x + ' . $labP . 'y <= ' . $labAvail . '` for labor, and `x + y <= ' . $slotAvail . '` for display space, with `x >= 0` and `y >= 0`.</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p><strong>Step 2. Build the initial simplex tableau.</strong> Each constraint gets its own slack variable: s1 for wax, s2 for labor, and s3 for display space.</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<table border="1" cellpadding="3"><tr><th>Basis</th><th>x</th><th>y</th><th>s1</th><th>s2</th><th>s3</th><th>Right side</th></tr>';
$solutionguide .= '<tr><td>s1</td><td>' . $waxV . '</td><td>' . $waxP . '</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>' . $waxAvail . '</td></tr>';
$solutionguide .= '<tr><td>s2</td><td>' . $labV . '</td><td>' . $labP . '</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>' . $labAvail . '</td></tr>';
$solutionguide .= '<tr><td>s3</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>' . $slotAvail . '</td></tr>';
$solutionguide .= '<tr><td>P</td><td>-' . $aProfit . '</td><td>-' . $bProfit . '</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr></table>';
$solutionguide .= '<p><strong>Step 3. Pivot until the profit row has no negative entries.</strong> The y entry is the most negative entry of the profit row, so y enters the basis. In the y column the ratio of the right side to the y entry is smallest in the wax row, so s1 leaves. In the new tableau the only negative profit-row entry left is the x entry, so x enters, and now the ratio test makes s2 leave. Every entry of the profit row is non-negative, so this tableau is optimal and the basic variables are x, y, and s3.</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p><strong>Step 4. Read the optimal solution.</strong> Because s1 and s2 are both non-basic, the wax row and the labor row are both tight at the optimum. Solving the pair</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p>`' . $waxV . 'x + ' . $waxP . 'y = ' . $waxAvail . '` and `' . $labV . 'x + ' . $labP . 'y = ' . $labAvail . '`</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p>gives `x = ' . $xopt . '` votive candles and `y = ' . $yopt . '` pillar candles. Check the wax: `' . $waxV . '*' . $xopt . ' + ' . $waxP . '*' . $yopt . ' = ' . $waxAvail . '` ounces, which is exactly what the shop has. Check the labor: `' . $labV . '*' . $xopt . ' + ' . $labP . '*' . $yopt . ' = ' . $labAvail . '` minutes, again exactly what the shop has. So no wax and no labor are wasted.</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p>The display row then gives `s3 = ' . $slotAvail . ' - ' . $xopt . ' - ' . $yopt . ' = ' . $slackSlots . '`, so ' . $slackSlots . ' display slots stay empty at the optimal plan.</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p>The maximum daily profit is `P = ' . $aProfit . '*' . $xopt . ' + ' . $bProfit . '*' . $yopt . ' = ' . $maxProfit . '` dollars.</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p><strong>Answer:</strong> make ' . $xopt . ' votive candles and ' . $yopt . ' pillar candles each day, for a maximum profit of ' . $maxProfit . ' dollars, with ' . $slackSlots . ' display slots left empty. All of the wax and all of the labor are used up, while the display case still has room, so it is the wax and the labor that limit production, not the display space.</p>';

