$solutionguide = "<p><b>Step 1 - Determinant.</b> Expand along the first row of A. The cofactors of the three entries in that row are `$c11`, `$c21` and `$c31`, so `det(A) = 1 * $c11 + $a12 * $c21 + $a13 * $c31 = 1`. The determinant is nonzero, so A is invertible. Because the determinant equals exactly 1, dividing the adjugate by it changes no entry, and every entry of A^{-1} is an integer.</p><p><b>Step 2 - Adjugate.</b> The entry in row i and column j of the adjugate is the cofactor of the entry in row j and column i of A. So the first row of the adjugate holds the cofactors of the first column of A, namely `$c11`, `$c12` and `$c13`, and the other rows are built the same way, giving</p><p>`A^{-1} = $AinvDisp`</p><p><b>Step 3 - Solve AX = B.</b> Multiplying both sides on the left by A^{-1} gives `X = A^{-1}B`. Multiply the inverse by the column B, row by column:</p><p>`X = $AinvDisp $Bdisp = $Xdisp`</p><p>Reading the column from top to bottom, `X = $Xlist`.</p><p><b>Check.</b> Row 1 of A dotted with X gives $b1, row 2 gives $b2, and row 3 gives $b3, which is exactly the column B, so the solution is confirmed.</p><p><b>Answer:</b> `A^{-1} = $AinvDisp` and `X = $Xlist`</p>";

