$solutionguide = '<p><strong>Step 1 - Find the means.</strong> Add the six call times for each rep. The times for Rep A are ' . $list1 . ', which add to ' . $sumAll . ' minutes, and ' . $sumAll . ' divided by 6 is ' . $mu . ' minutes. The times for Rep B are ' . $list2 . ', which also add to ' . $sumAll . ' minutes, so the mean for Rep B is also ' . $mu . ' minutes. The two groups have the same center.</p>';

$solutionguide .= '<p><strong>Step 2 - Find the sample standard deviations.</strong> Subtract the mean from each time, square the result, add the squares, and divide by n - 1 = 5. For Rep A the deviations are ' . $devlist1 . ' and their squares are ' . $sqlist1 . ', which add to ' . $ss1 . '. So s = sqrt of ' . $ss1 . ' over 5 = ' . $sd1r . ' minutes. For Rep B the deviations are ' . $devlist2 . ' and their squares are ' . $sqlist2 . ', which add to ' . $ss2 . '. So s = sqrt of ' . $ss2 . ' over 5 = ' . $sd2r . ' minutes.</p>';

$solutionguide .= '<p><strong>Step 3 - Compare and interpret.</strong> The means match but the spreads do not. ' . $moreCons . ' has a standard deviation of ' . $sdMin . ' minutes while ' . $lessCons . ' has a standard deviation of ' . $sdMax . ' minutes, roughly ' . $ratio . ' times as large. That large a gap means the call times for ' . $lessCons . ' scatter far from the mean, while the times for ' . $moreCons . ' stay close to it. So ' . $moreCons . ' is the more consistent rep.</p>';

$solutionguide .= '<p><strong>Step 4 - Why the shared mean is misleading.</strong> A mean reports only the center of a distribution, and it is the same for both reps, so it carries no information about how reliable either one is. The advertising sentence is true of both reps on average, but a caller cares about what usually happens, and that is exactly what the standard deviation measures. The call times for ' . $lessCons . ' run from ' . $rangeLess . ' minutes, while the call times for ' . $moreCons . ' run only from ' . $rangeMore . ' minutes. The mean alone hides that difference in consistency, so the rep whose data actually supports a promise of a similar experience every time is ' . $moreCons . ', and the rep whose data does not is ' . $lessCons . '.</p>';

$solutionguide .= '<p><strong>Answer:</strong> the shared mean is ' . $mu . ' minutes. Rep A has s = ' . $sd1r . ' minutes and Rep B has s = ' . $sd2r . ' minutes, so ' . $moreCons . ' is the more consistent rep. The identical means say nothing about spread, and the smaller standard deviation, not the mean, is the evidence that a caller gets a similar experience each time.</p>';

