$solutionguide = '<p><b>Step 1 - What the equal means say.</b> Both players average ' . $mean . ' points per round over ' . $rounds . ' rounds, so their ' . $rounds . '-round totals are the same and neither is the stronger scorer on average. The mean marks the balance point of the scores and nothing more. A player can reach ' . $mean . ' by scoring close to it every round, or by mixing very high rounds with very low ones.</p>';

$solutionguide .= '<p><b>Step 2 - What the standard deviations say.</b> The standard deviation measures the typical distance of a round score from that player\'s own mean, and it is measured in points, the same units as the scores. ' . $pBig . '\'s standard deviation is ' . $sdBig . ' points, far larger than the ' . $sdSmall . ' points for ' . $pSmall . '. So ' . $pBig . '\'s rounds scatter much farther from ' . $mean . ', some well above it and some well below it, while ' . $pSmall . '\'s rounds hug the mean. ' . $pSmall . ' is the more consistent player, and ' . $pBig . ' is the more variable one. A larger standard deviation is not a sign of a better player, only of a less predictable one.</p>';

$solutionguide .= '<p><b>Step 3 - The sponsor\'s two choices.</b> Both sets of scores are roughly symmetric and bell-shaped, so the standard deviation works as a measuring stick. The bonus line at ' . $target . ' points sits about ' . $zUpSmall . ' standard deviations above the mean for ' . $pSmall . ' but only about ' . $zUpBig . ' standard deviations above the mean for ' . $pBig . ', so a round of at least ' . $target . ' points is much more likely from ' . $pBig . '. The floor at ' . $low . ' points sits about ' . $zDnSmall . ' standard deviations below the mean for ' . $pSmall . ' and only about ' . $zDnBig . ' for ' . $pBig . ', so a round below ' . $low . ' is also much more likely from ' . $pBig . '. A large standard deviation cuts both ways: it makes the very high round possible and the very low round possible. To chase the bonus, pick ' . $pBig . '. To guard against a low round, pick ' . $pSmall . '.</p>';

