<p>A data set records $context. A histogram of these data is strongly skewed, so no particular shape can be assumed.</p>

<p><b>Part a.</b> Chebyshev's Rule guarantees that at least some percent of the values lie within `k` standard deviations of the mean. For `k = 2`, what percent is guaranteed? Give a percent rather than a proportion, so for example enter 75 for 75%. $answerbox[0]</p>

<p><b>Part b.</b> Repeat for `k = 3`. What percent of the values are guaranteed to lie within 3 standard deviations of the mean? Round to one decimal place if needed. $answerbox[1]</p>

<p><b>Part c.</b> Repeat for `k = 4.5`. What percent of the values are guaranteed to lie within 4.5 standard deviations of the mean? Round to one decimal place if needed. $answerbox[2]</p>

<p><b>Part d.</b> A classmate claims that Chebyshev's Rule tells you the exact percent of the values that lie within 2 standard deviations of the mean. Which statement best answers that claim? $answerbox[3]</p>

// === ANSWER ===
$solutionguide = '<p>Step 1 — Chebyshev\'s Rule says that for data of any shape, at least `1 - 1/k^2` of the values lie within `k` standard deviations of the mean. The rule gives a minimum, never the exact share.</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p>Step 2 — For `k = 2`: `1 - 1/4 = 0.75`, so at least 75% of the values lie within 2 standard deviations of the mean.</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p>Step 3 — For `k = 3`: `1 - 1/9 = 0.888...`, so at least 88.9% of the values lie within 3 standard deviations of the mean.</p>';
