// === QUESTION TEXT ===
<p>The $measure a single $item has mean `\mu = $mu` $unit and standard deviation `\sigma = $sigma` $unit. Assume the individual values are approximately normally distributed.</p>

<p>A random sample of `n = $n` $itemPl is selected, and `S` is the total of the `n` values in the sample.</p>

<p><b>a.</b> Find the mean of `S`, in $unit. $answerbox[0]</p>

<p><b>b.</b> Find the standard deviation of `S`, in $unit. Round to 4 decimal places if needed. $answerbox[1]</p>

<p><b>c.</b> Find the probability that `S` is greater than `$c` $unit. Give a decimal between 0 and 1, rounded to 4 decimal places. $answerbox[2]</p>

// === ANSWER ===
$solutionguide = '<p><b>Step 1 &mdash; Mean of the sum.</b> A sum of `n` independent observations has mean `n \mu`. So `\mu_S = ' . $n . ' \cdot ' . $mu . ' = ' . $muS . '` ' . $unit . '. Sums of many observations sit much farther out than single observations, which is why the total is centered at ' . $muS . ' rather than at ' . $mu . '.</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p><b>Step 2 &mdash; Standard deviation of the sum.</b> A sum of `n` independent observations has standard deviation `\sigma \sqrt{n}`, not `\sigma`. So `\sigma_S = ' . $sigma . ' \cdot \sqrt{' . $n . '} = ' . $sdDisp . '` ' . $unit . '. The spread grows like the square root of the sample size, so it grows more slowly than the center does.</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p><b>Step 3 &mdash; Probability.</b> The individual values are approximately normal, so `S` is approximately normal with mean ' . $muS . ' ' . $unit . ' and standard deviation ' . $sdDisp . ' ' . $unit . '. Convert the cutoff to a z-score: `z = (' . $c . ' - ' . $muS . ')/' . $sdDisp . ' = ' . $zDisp . '`. The area to the left of that z is ' . $cdfR . ', so the area to the right is `1 - ' . $cdfR . ' = ' . $probR . '`. Equivalently, use the normalcdf command with x = ' . $c . ', mean = ' . $muS . ', and standard deviation = ' . $sdDisp . ', then subtract the result from 1.</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p><b>Answer:</b> mean of `S` = ' . $muS . ' ' . $unit . '; standard deviation of `S` = ' . $sdDisp . ' ' . $unit . '; `P(S > ' . $c . ') = ' . $probR . '`.</p>';

